00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.910 align:start position:0%
the taliban shocked the world when it  took control of afghanistan but what may  surprise you is how they funded their  campaign  afghanistan is one of the poorest  countries in the world in march 2021  world bank described its economy as  shaped by fragility and aid dependence  but there is one surefire way to make  money in afghanistan the drug trade  afghanistan produces between 80 and 90  of the global supply of illegal opium a  key ingredient in heroin  opium production is an easy access it  feeds families it feeds  poor people it's a big business with  huge swathes of land dedicated to poppy  fields  it is estimated that one-fifth of  afghanistan's gdp comes from the opium  trade and the taliban play a part in  every aspect of it  they're involved in the growing  cultivating and trafficking of the drug  as well as taxing cultivators and drug  labs  and charging smugglers fees for  shipments not only do the farmers  conservative but some of their children  their family members then become heroin  addicts they will start using it and the  opium that comes and is sent over to  across europe also comes into different  communities in coral backgrounds in  britain for example my own entire  hamlets so it's a vicious circle  although it has been out of power since  2001  the taliban has always controlled  pockets of the country and in those  areas they introduced taxation policies  as well as taxes for travelling through  them  the taliban are also involved in the  illegal mining trade afghanistan is full  of extremely valuable minerals like  copper cobalt oil and gas  the taliban controls small extraction  sites as well as extorting money out of  other more legitimate ones  they also raise money through suspected  foreign investments from sympathetic  countries as well as looting areas they  take control of and keeping the spoils  of war  the taliban's takeover has sparked a  humanitarian crisis but also a  potentially huge economic one  afghanistan's economy is reliant on  foreign aid and grants and much of that  is up in the air  most countries around the world have  refused to recognize their government so  far  meaning receiving the same level of aid  or financial help is unlikely  the taliban have also promised to ditch  the opium trade  in an attempt to appear like a  legitimate government  but this leaves them with limited ways  of making money  there have been talks of foreign  investment in the country with china  reportedly keen to get involved in that  lucrative mineral business but this is  yet to actually lead to anything  concrete  another big problem is since the taliban  were removed from power in 2001  female employment has risen from zero  percent to 22 percent  the taliban leadership say they will let  women continue to work  but some have questioned whether this  will actually happen if women's right to  work is revoked then that takes a huge  portion of the tax-paying population  out of the workforce creating yet  another economic headache for  afghanistan's new leaders
 

